  "summary": "Astro Bot Rescue Mission is Japan Studio's virtual-reality platform adventure, released for PlayStation VR on 2 October 2018.",
  "body": [
    "Astro Bot Rescue Mission is Japan Studio's virtual-reality platform adventure, released for PlayStation VR on 2 October 2018. Captain Astro searches five worlds for his scattered robot crew, running and jumping under the player's control. The headset places the player inside the same space as the small hero, giving each platform and distant hiding place a physical scale. Finding a missing bot can mean looking around an obstacle or leaning towards a corner, making observation part of the journey alongside Astro's movement.",
    "Twenty levels arrange the search around different settings, with bosses closing each world. Rescued crew members help unlock those encounters, giving exploration a purpose beyond reaching an exit. The player controls Astro with a DualShock 4 while also watching the surrounding environment independently. That separation lets the hero continue along a narrow route as attention shifts towards something beside or behind it. A platformer's familiar question of where to go gains another dimension: the place worth investigating may sit outside the direction Astro is facing.",
    "The player's own presence also affects the world, from head movements that break obstacles to breath detected by the headset's microphone. Gadgets associated with the controller add further interactions to the stages. Kenneth C. M. Young's music accompanies the robots' expressive animation and changing situations. Directed by Nicolas Doucet, the game grew from characters introduced in The Playroom projects. Its central achievement is the partnership between the small character being guided and the much larger observer whose attention and movements help make the rescue possible."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A small robot in a world around the player",
    "Look beyond the obvious path",
    "The controller enters the rescue"
  ],
